v0.17.6 - Damage control priorities (tested)
--------------------------------------------
* Auto damage control picks the most urgent job instead of a random one in each group. Priority order: magazine fires, then flooding when the ship is near sinking, then fires next to magazines, by the torpedoes, in the hangar, in machinery, in command spaces and by the guns, then other fires and flooding, then knocked-out machinery, guns and fire control, then structural damage, and rebuilding last (DamageableSea.GetCoordsToRepair).
* Each dispatch writes a [DC] line to output_log.txt.


v0.17.5 - Balance and QOL
-------------------------
* Fires under control don't spread (tested): a fire with a damage control party fighting it can no longer spread to the compartments around it. Before, the party only stopped it burning its own compartment (DamageableSea.SpreadFires).
* Hold to add aircraft (tested): in Create New Air, holding + or - keeps changing the count until you let go, stopping at the most you can launch or at 0. A normal click still changes it by one (ModAirHold, CampaignInterface.OpenCreateAirStrike).
* Zero rebalance (tested): Zeros were losing about 20 to 1 against Wildcats. Zero toughness 18 -> 24, .50 hits 3 -> 2.5 (a Wildcat wing of three .50s now does 7.5 instead of 9), and 20 mm hits 6.5 -> 8. Head to head, Zeros and Wildcats now kill each other at about the same rate (DamageableAir).
* US bombers less tough, Zero nose guns harder-hitting (tested): a 72-plane US strike lost only 3 aircraft to 24 Zeros. Dauntless 36 -> 30, Helldiver 42 -> 36, Avenger 54 -> 44, Devastator 30 -> 26, and the Zero's fast-firing 7.7 mm nose guns 1 -> 1.5 per hit (DamageableAir).
* Fighters spread out (tested): once 2 fighters are on a target, others look elsewhere unless every other target is much further away. Before, CAP could pile 5+ fighters onto one bomber (EngagementAI.GetBestDogfightTarget).
* Carrier CAP follows its carrier (tested): AI carrier fighter cover now escorts its own task force on the campaign map instead of circling the spot it launched from, so it is overhead when a strike arrives (CampaignAI.OnRequestAirCover). Writes a [CAPFOLLOW] line to output_log.txt.
* Torpedo bombers don't give up when they lose sight of the target: they fly to where the ship was last seen and circle there. Flights on auto switch to another ship they can see. Before, they flew off to an old aim point without dropping (UnitAIAir.ModTorpedoSetup).


v0.17.4 - Balanced starting forces (tested)
-------------------------------------------
* Both sides start with the same forces in both campaigns: a carrier task force (1 carrier, 1 battleship, 2 cruisers, 5 destroyers), a cruiser task force (1 heavy cruiser, 1 light cruiser, 3 destroyers), 3 destroyer patrols of 2-4 ships, and 2-3 single submarines near the middle of the map.
* Your starting ships are the historical August 1942 ones: Enterprise's group and San Francisco's cruisers for the US, Shokaku's group and Chokai's cruisers for Japan. Your destroyer patrols are a random mix (CampaignAI.CreateStandingMissions).
* AI carrier and cruiser task forces no longer respawn for free once sunk. Destroyer and submarine patrols still do (sea_missions.txt).
* Each of your starting groups writes a [STARTFLEET] line to output_log.txt.
* Needs a new campaign.


v0.17.3 - Air Rework (tested)
-----------------------------
* Toughness instead of 3 hits: every aircraft type now has its own toughness, based on its real size, structure, armour plate and self-sealing tanks. It smokes at 1/3 damage, smokes twice at 2/3, and goes down at full damage. Before, every plane went down on its 3rd hit, from a Zero to a B-17 (DamageableAir.ApplySmallCalibreDamageToAir).
* Real gun calibres: 7.7 mm / .30 hits do 1, .50 hits do 3 and 20 mm hits do 6.5. Each bullet now does its damage once, instead of counting as several hits (AAAParticleCollisions.OnParticleCollision).
* Zeros no longer shred everything: the Zero's guns used to count each bullet as up to 2 hits, against 1 for a whole wing of Wildcat .50s. Now a Wildcat's 3 guns per wing hit far harder, and US planes with armour and self-sealing tanks take a lot of 7.7 mm punishment. Zeros still burn after a few hits (WeaponParticle.Start).
* Armour depends on calibre: pilot armour stops most rifle-calibre hits, some .50s and few cannon shells.
* Rear gunners by calibre: 7.7 mm on Japanese bombers, twin .30 on Dauntless and Helldiver, .50 on B-17 and B-25 (WeaponAAA.InitialiseAAA).
* Ship light AA does 7 per hit, so it stays about as deadly to Japanese aircraft and a bit less to the tougher US types.
* Heavy AA: a burst within 10 m does 40 damage instead of an instant kill. It still kills Japanese aircraft, Wildcats and Dauntlesses, but Hellcats, Avengers and bombers can survive it (DamageableAir.ApplyDamageToAIR).
* Bombs and torpedoes were checked against their real explosive charges and left unchanged.
* Bigger air battles: up to 72 aircraft per side in a battle at once (was 48) (config.txt maxAirPerSide).
* Debug log lines ([AIRDMG]) show each plane's guns, toughness and armour when it spawns, and every smoke and kill.


v0.17.2 - Fighter AI: escorts and CAP (tested)
----------------------------------------------
* Escorts protect their bombers: while their side still has loaded bombers, fighters go for enemy fighters first, above all those attacking their bombers. They mostly leave enemy bombers alone, and lose interest in planes more than 5 km from the bombers they protect (EngagementAI.GetBestDogfightTarget).
* Defending fighters (CAP) go for the bombers: loaded torpedo bombers first, then dive bombers, then level bombers. Planes within 10 km of their own ships come first, and bombers that have already dropped are mostly ignored.
* Self-defence: a fighter strongly prefers a plane that is attacking it.
* Spreading out: each fighter already on a target makes it less attractive to the next one, so fighters split up instead of piling on. A fighter slightly prefers its current target, so it doesn't keep switching.
* No more dogpile: when AI escorts spot one of your planes, each AI fighter now picks its own target, instead of every AI fighter in the battle chasing the one plane spotted (EngagementAI.OnDetectUnit).
* Applies to both sides, including your fighters on auto.
* Every pick writes a [FIGHTER] line to output_log.txt.


v0.17.1 - Air strike AI
-----------------------
* Real dive bombing: dive bombers enter battle at about 2,000-2,200 m and push over 1,100-1,200 m from the target, for a steep dive with release at 450-550 m (data files plus EngagementData.CreateUnitGroup).
* Auto attack: your bomber flights with no orders pick their own ship and attack. Manual orders and waypoints always win (UnitAIAir.ModCheckAutoStrike).
* Smarter target choice (both sides): carriers first, then battleships and cruisers. Flights spread over the big ships instead of all piling onto one, torpedo bombers avoid destroyers, and sinking ships are ignored (EngagementAI.ModPickStrikeTarget).
* Break off: a flight whose target is already sinking switches ship before it commits to its run.
* Hammer and anvil: torpedo flights on the same ship split to both bows. The far-side flight goes round well ahead of the ship, and the flights wait for each other (up to 300 s) before all turning in together (UnitAIAir.ModTorpedoSetup).
* Units fix: battle distances are in 10 m game units. The formation break (1,200 m), torpedo grouping (3 km), sub ambush (2.5 km), sub return (600 m) and minimum torpedo distance (150 m) now work at the intended distances, and F10 shows real metres.
* Debug log lines ([AIRAI], [TORP]) are written to output_log.txt and stay in the release.
* Polish: torpedo flights only start waiting within 5 km of their set-up point and remember their assigned side, so a lost flight no longer leaves its partner waiting the full 300 s. The [TORP] log is limited to one line every 5 seconds.


v0.17 - Smarter AI: weighs the odds
-----------------------------------
* Before chasing one of your task forces, an AI force compares strength: warship tonnage, with carriers counted x1.5 and damaged ships counted less (CampaignAI.WarshipTonnage, ShouldChaseSpotted).
* It counts its own strength plus every other aggressive AI force within 200 km of your fleet, since they converge and join the same battle.
* It only attacks if that combined strength is at least 75% of yours; otherwise it carries on with its mission (CampaignAI.GetNewSpottedWaypoint).
* Merchants, oilers and submarines don't count as strength, so the AI still hunts lone supply ships.


v0.16 - Faster battle time
--------------------------
* The battle speed button cycles x1 > x2 > x5 > x10 (was x1 / x5). The top speed comes from config.txt timeCompressionFactor (now 10) (CombatInterface.SetTimeCompression).
* Speeding up is still blocked during an incoming air attack.
* The physics step stays at the x5 size, so hits stay accurate at x10.


v0.15 - Lasting damage
----------------------
* Auto damage control skips equipment that needs a port repair, and rebuilds destroyed hull compartments once nothing else needs doing (DamageableSea.GetCoordsToRepair).
* Knocked-out equipment stays broken between battles: guns, engine rooms, propellers and other equipment that end a battle knocked out (red) stay offline until the ship is repaired at a port. Before, the game switched them back on for free after every battle (EngagementData.GetCellVariables).
* Damage parties can't bring that carried-over equipment back online at sea. Equipment knocked out in the new battle can still be repaired (DamageableSea.RepairCompartment, GetRepairTime).
* Knocked-out engine rooms and propellers also keep the task force slow on the campaign map.
* Applies to the AI too.


v0.14 - Night fighting (pending test)
-------------------------------------
* Fires act as a detection beacon: a burning ship is much easier to spot at night (x1.45 with one fire, up to x1.8), and slightly easier by day from the smoke (x1.1). This works both ways (SensorManager.GetVisibilityModifier).
* Darker nights: night visibility is 25% of daylight (was 50%), so lookouts see about 7 km instead of 15 km. Dawn and dusk are slightly darker (config.txt visibilityByHour).
* Light matters: ships lit by star shells or searchlights are seen 80% further (was 20%).
* Closer night encounters: night battles start at up to 10 km (was 15) (config.txt nightEncounterMaxRange).
* Radar is unchanged, so radar-equipped ships gain a real night advantage.


v0.13 - Air strike packages
---------------------------
* One wave: a strike's flights spread out side by side instead of trailing behind each other, so the whole strike arrives together (EngagementData.BreakUpAirGroup).
* Mixed strike packages: Create New Air counters start at 0. Every type you raise with + launches when you press Done, and the faster groups escort the slowest so the package flies as one. The highlighted type uses the chosen payload; the others use their standard load.


v0.12 - AI fleets hold formation in battle
------------------------------------------
* AI surface ships keep their formation place while attacking, instead of each charging its own target. The leader steers, the group moves as one, and guns and torpedoes fire as normal (UnitAISea.AttackUnit, KeepStationInBattle).
* Destroyers still break off for torpedo runs, then rejoin.
* Formation breaks into a melee when an enemy is within 1,200 m, or when the leader is sunk or retreating (the group forms up on the game's new leader).


v0.11 - Bigger air strikes and multi task force battles
-------------------------------------------------------
* Up to 24 aircraft per launch (was 12), for you and the AI's carrier strikes (config.txt maxInFlight).
* Up to 48 aircraft per side in a battle at once (was 24) (config.txt maxAirPerSide).
* Multi task force battles (pincers): every task force from either side within 50 km joins the battle (before: only one surface force per side), up to 40 ships per side, each starting from its real direction (CampaignManager.GetValidNeighbouringMobiles).
* No surface cooldown after battle: setup.txt postEngageSeaCooldown 1800 -> 0, so forces can fight or reinforce again straight away.


v0.10 - Large task forces and new formations
--------------------------------------------
* Task forces can hold up to 20 ships (was 10), for you and the AI (setup.txt maxShipsInTaskForce).
* All six formations now have places for 25 ships. The first 10 places are unchanged, so ships 11+ no longer start stacked on the leader.
* Three new formations:
  * Battle Line + Screen: capital ships in line, destroyer columns on both flanks.
  * Cruising Disposition: carriers or battleships in the middle, two rings of escorts.
  * Convoy Box: transports and oilers in a 3x3 box, escorts round the outside.
* Bigger AI fleets in both campaigns:
  * Surface Intercept and Bombard groups: 14-16 ships (was 6-9).
  * Carrier groups: 16-18 ships (was 9-10).
  * Invasions and convoys: 14-15 ships (same transports, more escorts).
  * Large fast troop runs: 14 ships (was 10).
* AI fleets use the formation that suits them and list their ships in formation order.


v0.9 - Combat damage package
----------------------------
* Damaged ships slow their task force on the campaign map. Destroyed propellers or engine rooms reduce a ship's speed (never below 25%), and the force moves at its slowest ship's speed. It is recalculated after every battle and when ships join or leave a force.
* List and capsize: uneven flooding makes a ship list. At 60% of its sinking limit it capsizes and sinks.
* Automatic counter-flooding with a safety net: the crew floods a compartment on the high side to level the ship. It never takes total flooding above 80% of the sinking limit, and pumps its own water out if the list swings back. AI ships always; your ships when Auto damage control is on.
* Water and fire: a flooded compartment can't catch fire, and flooding a burning compartment puts the fire out.
* Fire no longer floods the compartment it is burning in.
* Bigger fires spread faster: each burning fire adds 10% to the spread chance, up to triple.
* Destroyed hull compartments can be rebuilt at sea: 10 minutes on a destroyer up to 30 minutes on a battleship. Destroyed deck items (guns, AA, bridge) stay destroyed until port repair, as a balance choice.
* Harder damage control (config): fires burn hotter (15 per tick instead of 10), fires and flooding take 180 s instead of 120 s to fight, and both spread a little more easily.


v0.8 - Torpedoes and fuel warnings
----------------------------------
* Torpedo coordination: AI destroyers and cruisers within 3 km of each other hold fire and launch together, so their spreads arrive at the same time. A ship fires anyway after 40 seconds, or when its target comes within 60% of torpedo range.
* Second torpedo salvo: AI surface ships can attack with torpedoes again once their tubes are half reloaded (before, they only ever fired once per battle).
* Type 93 "Long Lance": decisive against destroyers. It no longer breaks a ship's back above about 5,150 t (config torpBreakBackValues).
* LOW / EMPTY fuel flags on the task force hover panel.


v0.7 - Smarter AI submarines
----------------------------
* Subs ambush from 1,000-1,600 m ahead and 400-800 m to the side (was 150-250 m).
* The ambush starts when one of your surface ships comes within 2,500 m.
* Split salvos: a sub divides its ready tubes between up to 3 targets, most valuable first (for example 3 / 2 / 1).
* Hit, run and reload: after firing, a sub slips away quietly for 2 minutes, then reloads. After 4 minutes, with tubes ready and no ship within 600 m, it comes back for another attack.
* A damaged sub never comes back.


v0.6 - Rules popups and tester tools
------------------------------------
* Rules popups: 5 pages with custom images in both campaigns (Ship Orders, Shipyards and Economy, Repair and Fuel, Veteran Crews and Home Ports, Testers!).
* F9 - Reveal enemies (testers): shows every enemy task force on the campaign map. It only changes what you see.
* F10 - Developer view (testers): a battle panel listing each enemy ship's distance, depth, speed in real knots, and AI action. Press Tab to bring back the interface.


v0.5 - Strategic AI v1
----------------------
* More persistent pursuit: the AI tries to intercept spotted fleets on 50% of its decisions (was 25%).
* Supply-line raids: the AI hunts single spotted merchants and oilers (before, only groups of more than two).
* Smarter bombardment: 60% of the time the AI bombards your highest-level port or airfield instead of a random one.


v0.4 - Veteran crews and home ports
-----------------------------------
* Veteran crews: ships gain experience for each battle survived.
  * Green: 0-1 battles.
  * Trained: 2-4 battles, +4% efficiency.
  * Veteran: 5-8 battles, +8% efficiency.
  * Elite: 9 or more battles, +12% efficiency.
* Higher efficiency means faster reloads and damage control. Promotions are announced on the map, and a sunk ship loses its experience.
* Hover panel: shows each ship's crew rank and fuel.
* Home ports: a ship belongs to the shipyard that ordered it and can only be recruited there. Disbanding at a shipyard makes that its new home port, and a captured home port blocks recruiting until you retake it.


v0.3 - Repair and fuel
----------------------
* Repair cost: releasing a damaged ship at a port costs command points in proportion to the damage, up to half the ship's cost. The full refund still arrives when repairs finish, and higher-level ports repair faster.
* Fuel system:
  * Every task force carries 10 days of fuel. It drains while sailing, and an empty force can only crawl.
  * The Rearm order refuels at a port (level 3 or higher, or a home port) from the port's stockpile.
  * It also refuels at sea from an oiler or supply ship carrying fuel, in calm weather during operating hours.
  * Only home ports start with fuel.


v0.2 - Shipyards and economy
----------------------------
* Only the main base (Rabaul for Japan) can build ships.
* Port level limits ship size:
  * Destroyers: level 1.
  * Light cruisers: level 2.
  * Heavy cruisers: level 3.
  * Battleships, battlecruisers and light carriers: level 4.
  * Fleet carriers: level 5.
* Command points:
  * At least 75 a week, plus points for every location, port rank and airfield rank held.
  * Sinking an enemy ship pays half its cost.
  * Battleships and carriers cost twice as much.


v0.1 - Ship ordering
--------------------
* Every ship (apart from the starting task forces) must be ordered before use.
* Deposits: ordering costs a 50% deposit in command points, which isn't refunded.
* Build times:
  * Destroyers: 7 days.
  * Submarines: 10 days.
  * Light cruisers: 14 days.
  * Heavy cruisers: 18 days.
  * Light carriers: 30 days.
  * Battleships: 35 days.
  * Fleet carriers: 42 days.
* Ships that did not exist yet arrive on their historical date.
* Delivered ships wait at the ordering shipyard; recruit them there for their full cost.
* Pending orders are listed in the Campaign Summary (F5), ORDERS & REPAIR tab.
* One combined message lists all ships delivered on the same day.


